# Line-ending policy: LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# This working tree is not a private checkout. Drive L: on Windows is an SMB
# mount of /mnt/main/homelab on the NAS, so these exact bytes are what Docker,
# Traefik, Blocky and the rest read at runtime. LF is a runtime requirement
# here, not a style preference.
#
# That is why every rule below says `eol=lf` rather than just `text=auto`.
# `text=auto` alone normalises what goes *into* the repo but still lets Git for
# Windows write CRLF into the working tree on checkout. `eol=lf` pins both.
#
# Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` at the *system* level, while WSL's
# git has nothing set. Two gits, one shared working tree, opposite rules — that
# is what produced ~40 phantom "modified" files. .gitattributes overrides
# core.autocrlf for both, so they now agree. Keep this file committed; the
# per-clone git config cannot travel and will not protect a fresh clone.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Called out explicitly — these are the ones where CRLF actively breaks things
# rather than merely creating diff noise. A CRLF shebang makes the kernel look
# for an interpreter named "/bin/sh\r", which fails with a confusing ENOENT.
*.sh         text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf

# PowerShell is Windows-only, but PS 5.1 and 7 both parse LF without complaint,
# so it stays on the single repo-wide rule rather than adding a second axis of
# drift. Change to `eol=crlf` if a signed-script or ISE workflow ever needs it.
*.ps1        text eol=lf

# Safety net so a future binary asset is never mangled by normalisation.
# `text=auto` already detects most of these, but being explicit is free.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.db   binary
*.sqlite binary
